A dividing line is 3 or more horizontal characters (any one of - = _ *)

=================

> Additions beyond the original sketch

// NOTE: everything below this heading was added while implementing the
// parser; see help/mepml.org for the full reference.

// INFO: subscript is `,,x,,` rather than `_x_` because `_x_` is already underline.

// WARNING: a line starting with `>` and a space is always a heading, so
// `>big<` text at the very start of a line must not have a space after the `>`.

Lists use -, * or + for bullets and 1. or 1) for numbers; indent to nest:

- first item with *bold* inside
- second item
  - a nested item
  - [ ] an open task
  - [x] a finished task
1. numbered
2. and another

Escape any marker with a backslash: \*not bold\*, \$5 and \$6, \\. A lone
dollar amount like $5 is never math. Inline text // a trailing comment is hidden.

Tables can align columns with colons in the separator row, and the pipes
don't need to line up in the source (mep lines the columns up when drawing):

| Left | Centre | Right |
| :--- | :----: | ----: |
| a | b | 1 |
| `c` | *d* | 2.50 |
| a longer cell | >big< | 100 |
@caption{An aligned table}

Code blocks take options in the header or from //? lines right above them,
and a ```lang header works too. Results are written back between the markers
when the block is run (C-c C-c in mep):

```{python, eval=true}
print(sum(range(10)))
```

```r
x <- 1:10
mean(x)
```

A block with a file= option draws a figure: R and Python have their plotting
pointed at that file, and after a run the image is shown in the block's
results, numbered and captioned like any other figure:

```{r, file="squares.png"}
x <- 1:10
plot(x, x^2, type = "b", main = "Squares")
```
@caption{Squares, drawn by R}
@alttext{A line plot of x squared for x from 1 to 10}

A block with results=html prints HTML: its results keep that markup, and mep
draws it in place of the text (move the cursor into it to see the markup).
The exports embed it as HTML:

```{python, results=html}
rows = [("apple", 3), ("banana", 5), ("cherry", 12)]
print("<p><b>Fruit</b> counted by <i>Python</i>:</p>")
print("<table>")
print("<tr><th>Fruit</th><th>Count</th></tr>")
for name, n in rows:
    print(f"<tr><td>{name}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>")
print("</table>")
```
// result_begin: html
// <p><b>Fruit</b> counted by <i>Python</i>:</p>
// <table>
// <tr><th>Fruit</th><th>Count</th></tr>
// <tr><td>apple</td><td>3</td></tr>
// <tr><td>banana</td><td>5</td></tr>
// <tr><td>cherry</td><td>12</td></tr>
// </table>
// result_end

An exec block runs a program in a terminal inside its results: C-c C-c starts
it, Enter (or a click) types into it, Ctrl-\ Ctrl-N comes back to the text,
and C-c C-k (or the stop button) stops it. When it ends, its last screen is
kept as the results:

```{exec, rows=8}
python3 -q
```
// result_begin: terminal
// >>> print(6*7, [c.upper() for c in "mep"])
// 42 ['M', 'E', 'P']
// >>>
// result_end

With results=exec, a block in any language is the program: it is compiled
(when the language is) and then run in the same kind of terminal:

```{c, results=exec, rows=6}
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    char name[64];
    printf("What is your name? ");
    fflush(stdout);
    if (fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin)) printf("Hello, %s", name);
    return 0;
}
```
// result_begin: terminal
// What is your name? Ada
// Hello, Ada
// result_end

An exec-gui block runs a program with a window of its own, and shows that
window inside the results (on X11): Enter or a click hands it the keyboard,
Ctrl-\ takes it back, and when it closes its last picture is kept:

```{exec-gui, rows=12}
xeyes
```
// result_begin: gui
// [running] xeyes
// result_end

```{python, results=exec-gui, rows=10}
import math
import tkinter as tk

root = tk.Tk()
root.title("smile")
canvas = tk.Canvas(root, bg="black", highlightthickness=0)
canvas.pack(fill="both", expand=True)

def draw(event=None):
    canvas.delete("all")
    w, h = canvas.winfo_width(), canvas.winfo_height()
    a = min(w * 0.38, h * 1.6)  # half the smile's width
    b = h * 0.4                 # how deep it curves
    cx, cy = w / 2, h * 0.18
    line = max(4, h * 0.03)

    def lip(x):  # the smile's height at x
        return cy + b * math.sqrt(max(0.0, 1 - ((x - cx) / a) ** 2))

    # The tongue, out of the smile halfway to its right end, leaning that way.
    tx = cx + a / 2
    tw, tl = h * 0.24, h * 0.34
    lean = tw * 0.35
    left, right = tx - tw / 2, tx + tw / 2
    pts = [left, lip(left), left, lip(left),
           left + lean * 0.2, lip(tx) + tl * 0.7,
           tx + lean * 0.6, lip(tx) + tl,
           right + lean, lip(tx) + tl * 0.62,
           right, lip(right), right, lip(right)]
    canvas.create_polygon(pts, smooth=True, fill="white", outline="white")
    canvas.create_line(tx + lean * 0.05, lip(tx) + line * 1.5, tx + lean * 0.35, lip(tx) + tl * 0.55,
                       fill="black", width=max(2, line * 0.4), capstyle="round")
    # The smile, drawn over the tongue's root.
    canvas.create_arc(cx - a, cy - b, cx + a, cy + b, start=190, extent=160,
                      style="arc", outline="white", width=line)

canvas.bind("<Configure>", draw)
root.mainloop()
```
// result_begin: gui
// [running] python program
// result_end

